# speed-default-level — what SPEED should default to now that it works

STATUS: DONE — pjb confirmed the implemented default 2026-08-28.

## The question

The debugger-public-interface issue gives the default qualities as

    ((DEBUG 3) (SPACE 0) (SPEED 0))

and notes, right above the fork matrix: "until we have a compiler, SPEED
is always at level 0". That parenthetical is the whole difficulty. The
0 was not a considered default for a working SPEED — it was a statement
that SPEED had nothing to reach.

Now it has. So the documented default has to be re-decided rather than
copied.

## What I chose, and why

    ((DEBUG 3) (SPACE 0) (SPEED 2))

Two reasons.

It is the setting that reproduces the behaviour the engine already had
while SPEED was pinned. Since 2.0.3 functions compile once hot, and
since 2.0.7 their instrumented forks do too. Under the new algebra that
is exactly SPEED 2. Shipping SPEED 0 as the default would have turned
the compiler off for everyone as a side effect of giving it a switch —
a performance regression wearing spec-compliance as a disguise, and the
kind of change nobody would think to look for.

And it reads correctly as a statement of priorities, which is what the
levels are for: debugging matters most (DEBUG 3), speed next (SPEED 2),
size not at all (SPACE 0). That is clautolisp's posture.

## What pjb might prefer instead

  - SPEED 0, taking the issue's default literally. Then compilation is
    opt-in, and the first thing every user does is turn it on. Argues
    for it: the compiler is new, and a default that never compiles is
    the conservative one while it settles.

  - SPEED 3, compiling everything at load. Argues for it: it is the
    setting under which file-wide optimizations happen, so it is what
    the compiler is eventually FOR. Argues against it: it makes loading
    a file pay to compile functions that may never be called, which is
    precisely the cost the hotness threshold exists to avoid.

  - Different defaults for the interactive REPL and for -Efile / batch
    runs. A REPL wants fast startup; a batch run wants fast code. This
    is the answer I would reach for if the single default proves wrong
    in either direction, and it needs no change to the algebra — only a
    different CLAL-OPTIMIZE at start-up per mode.

## Where to change it

One place: *CLAL-OPTIMIZATION*'s initial value in
clautolisp/autolisp-builtins-core/source/api.lisp. The runtime picks it
up through APPLY-CLAL-OPTIMIZATION, which INSTALL-CORE-BUILTINS calls, so
there is no second default to keep in step.

The documented default appears in three places that must move with it:
the CLAL-OPTIMIZATION spec entry, the user manual section, and the
release-notes entry.


DECISION: the implemented default ((DEBUG 3) (SPACE 0) (SPEED 2)) is ok.
The issue can be closed.
